tion/worked-solution": "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)\\\\ q(x)&=- 2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)\\\\ n&=2.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{2}$ and set $z=y^{-1}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{1}{y^{2}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(- 3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)\\right)z=2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right).$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int - 3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{- \\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{- \\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}}\\,z\\right)=2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right) \\cdot e^{- \\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{- \\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}}z=- \\frac{2 \\cdot e^{- \\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}}}{3}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{\\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}} - \\frac{2}{3}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{-1}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\frac{3}{3 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{\\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}} - 2}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals.",
